Applying mass-energy conservation, the total disintegration energy $Q$ released in the decay is $Q = (m_n - m_p - m_e)c^2$. Substituting the rest mass values: $Q = (939.565 - 938.272 - 0.511)	ext{ MeV} = 0.782	ext{ MeV} = 782.0	ext{ keV}$. Because the proton mass is much greater than the electron and neutrino masses ($m_p \approx 1836 m_e$), the recoil kinetic energy of the proton is virtually zero, meaning the energy $Q$ is shared entirely between the electron and the antineutrino: $Q = K_e + E_{ ar{
u}}$. Substituting $E_{ ar{
u}} = 282.0	ext{ keV}$, we get $K_e = Q - E_{ ar{
u}} = 782.0	ext{ keV} - 282.0	ext{ keV} = 500.0	ext{ keV}$. Taking the maximum electron kinetic energy without subtracting the neutrino energy gives $782.0	ext{ keV}$; subtracting from the neutrino energy gives $218.0	ext{ keV}$; adding the neutrino energy gives $1064.0	ext{ keV}$.

Applying Moseley's law for $K_\alpha$ emission, $\sqrt{
u} = a(Z - 1)$, taking the ratio of frequencies for the unknown element $X$ and manganese ($Z_{	ext{Mn}} = 25$) yields $ rac{\sqrt{
u_X}}{\sqrt{
u_{	ext{Mn}}}} =  rac{Z_X - 1}{Z_{	ext{Mn}} - 1} =  rac{Z_X - 1}{25 - 1} =  rac{Z_X - 1}{24}$. Substituting the measured frequencies gives $\sqrt{ rac{1.884 	imes 10^{18}	ext{ Hz}}{1.420 	imes 10^{18}	ext{ Hz}}} = \sqrt{1.32676} \approx 1.15185$. Equating this to the atomic number expression yields $ rac{Z_X - 1}{24} = 1.15185 \implies Z_X - 1 = 24 	imes 1.15185 \approx 27.64 \approx 28$. Solving for the atomic number gives $Z_X = 28 + 1 = 29$, which corresponds to Copper ($	ext{Cu}$). Omitting the screening constant $b = 1$ yields $Z_X = 28$; taking square of ratios directly yields $30$; using arithmetic average yields $27$.
